# Copy me to `.security-terms` and add your terms. I am committed; that file is
# gitignored and never leaves your machine.
#
#     cp .security-terms.example .security-terms
#
# WHAT THIS IS
# A list of literal strings that must never appear in this repository. The
# pre-commit hook greps every staged file for each one and blocks the commit on
# a match. One term per line. Case-insensitive, substring match, punctuation and
# spaces are fine. Lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT COMMITTED
# The main term is the client's name. Writing it into a file in a public repo
# would put it in the repository this exists to keep it out of (SEC-1).
#
# WHAT TO PUT IN IT
# Realistically the first two lines are most of the value.
#
#   1. The organization's name, plus anything it is also called - a short form,
#      an abbreviation, a division that is recognisable on its own.
#   2. Real program or project names from the source documents.
#   3. Real people named in the source documents.
#   4. Internal system or platform names that were genericised in transcription.
#      Two of those are already in docs/process/work-authorization-flow.md,
#      appearing there as "trading partner number" and "finance / ERP entity
#      code" - the real names belong here.
#   5. Specific site locations, cost center codes or CAGE codes you know appear
#      in the source material. Generic patterns already catch most of these
#      shapes, so this is a backstop rather than the point.
#
# WHAT NOT TO PUT IN IT
# The fictional names. "Calderis Aerospace" and the department names under
# docs/reference/ are invented on purpose and are meant to be in the repo.
# Adding one here blocks every commit until you take it out again.
#
# A filled-in file is just a list, and can be as short as four lines:
#
#     <the organization's full name>
#     <the short form everyone actually says>
#     <its abbreviation, if it has one>
#     <a program name from the source documents>
#
# Deliberately written as descriptions rather than as plausible fake names. This
# file is scanned like every other committed file - which is what catches a real
# name accidentally typed here - so a realistic-looking illustration would flag
# itself the moment somebody used it as a term.
#
# Add yours below this line.
